    <div class="gls-methodology">
      <h3>Concepts</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Long / Short</strong> — betting price goes up (long) or down (short).</li>
        <li><strong>Perp</strong> — perpetual futures contract; tracks an asset's price without expiry.</li>
        <li><strong>Spot</strong> — the actual asset (real coins), not a derivative.</li>
        <li><strong>Leverage</strong> — using borrowed capital to amplify a position.</li>
        <li><strong>Liquidation</strong> — forced close of a leveraged position that hit its margin limit.</li>
        <li><strong>Funding rate</strong> — periodic payment between longs and shorts to keep perp price near spot.</li>
        <li><strong>Open interest (OI)</strong> — total value of outstanding leveraged positions.</li>
        <li><strong>CVD</strong> — Cumulative Volume Delta; net aggressive (taker) buying minus selling.</li>
        <li><strong>TWAP</strong> — Time-Weighted Average Price; a scheduled order that executes evenly over time.</li>
        <li><strong>Assistance Fund (AF)</strong> — Hyperliquid's protocol fund that auto-buys HYPE from trading fees.</li>
        <li><strong>Unstaking queue</strong> — coins waiting to unlock from staking (forward sell-pressure overhang).</li>
        <li><strong>Basis</strong> — the gap between spot and futures prices; signals crowding.</li>
        <li><strong>Book imbalance</strong> — ratio of resting buy vs sell orders; hints at near-term pressure.</li>
        <li><strong>Long/short ratio</strong> — proportion of accounts positioned long vs short (crowd positioning).</li>
        <li><strong>IC (Information Coefficient)</strong> — Spearman rank correlation between a signal's value and future price return. Measures predictive power.</li>
        <li><strong>Hit rate</strong> — fraction of times the signal's direction matched the subsequent price move.</li>
        <li><strong>BPS</strong> — basis points; 1 bps = 0.01%.</li>
        <li><strong>PP</strong> — percentage points; the arithmetic difference between two percentages.</li>
        <li><strong>Confidence</strong> — stays LOW until signals are IC-proven (|IC| &#8805; 0.10 over enough hourly pairs). HIGH means the signals have demonstrated predictive power.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div id="gls-entries" style="padding:0 var(--sp-4)"></div>

    <div class="gls-methodology" id="gls-methodology">
      <h3>Reading the data</h3>

      <h3>The bias</h3>
      <p>The bias is an IC-weighted vote of the signals for a chosen horizon, shown with a confidence flag that stays <strong>LOW until signals are IC-proven</strong> (|IC| &#8805; 0.10 over enough hourly pairs). Weak IC means the signal doesn't predict price here — that's a finding, not a bug.</p>

      <h3>The contrarian convention</h3>
      <p>Crowd/retail positioning and exchange-deposit signals read <em>contrarian</em> (high = bearish, fade). Order flow, smart-money positioning, accumulation, and withdrawals read <em>with</em> the move (high = bullish).</p>

      <h3>Cross-cutting data caveats</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>CEX OI and HL OI are <strong>separate pools</strong> — never summed.</li>
        <li>CEX and HL liquidations are <strong>separate pools</strong> — never summed; magnitudes undercount.</li>
        <li>L/S ratios count heads, not capital — laggy and gameable.</li>
        <li>Order-book depth/imbalance is gameable (spoofable walls).</li>
        <li>CVD is taker (aggressor) flow only; the maker side is invisible.</li>
        <li>Holder concentration excludes flagged system wallets (overstated if that list is incomplete).</li>
        <li>TWAP / AF / unstake-pipeline metrics are warming until the native indexer lands.</li>
        <li>Execution is testnet; all market data is read from mainnet. On-chain layer is HYPE-only.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

